"L" <L_1_L_0@yahoo.com> wrote:

> Also, corridors can now occasionally contain
> objects, traps and/or graffiti.

Traps in corridors change the whole texture of the
game, since that could include a trapdoor or hole,
and, if the PC had no way to dig around it, leave a
corridor unpassable.

Random objects generated in corridors (depending on
how random objects are created, some random number
per level, or some random chance per grid square
(the latter, I suspect), change the game balance
quite a bit by increasing the initially generated
object richness of each level; corridors are a big
fraction of all the "non-rock" grid squares per
level, the equivalent of several extra rooms.

Also, less importantly, you've now spoiled the
chance to freely identify a scroll of teleportation
as the "only scroll usually found" randomly
initially generated in a corridor (really a doorless
closet that happens to adjoin a corridor).

[...]

> A consequence of this is that "secret corridor
> squares" are no longer randomly generated - except
> on the rogue level. Personally I don't consider it
> that big a loss - secret doors were always the
> more troublesome of the pair.

I'll respectfully disagree. Between the facts that
there are lots of _real_ dead-end corridors
generated, making it uncertain whether searching at
the end of an existing known corrior will _ever_
succeed, and the fact that searching for a single
hidden door/corridor grid square can take dozens of
turns, you've made a huge difference in how many
sustinance points it consumes to explore a level,
decreasing that requirement, where before, giving
searching up on what might be a real corridor, to
save sustinance level, was a necessary part of play
strategy.

Considering these issues, I think your changes need
to be rethought and tuned with an eye toward
maintaining game balance.

